          %title
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong
              connective differential graded
              = succeed "-ring" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{C}^\infty
            is a (homologically graded with nonnegative degrees) real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+differential+graded+algebra"} commutative differential graded algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with a structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-ring"} C^∞-ring
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0
          %p
            A
            %strong
              coconnective differential graded
              = succeed "-ring" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{C}^\infty
            is a (cohomologically graded with nonnegative degrees) real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+differential+graded+algebra"} commutative differential graded algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with a structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-ring"} C^∞-ring
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0
            such that the differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\colon A_0\to A_1
            in degree 0 is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-derivation"} C^∞-derivation
          %p
            In the unbounded case,
            %a{:href => "#CR"} Carchedi–Roytenberg
            proposed the following definition:
          %p
            An
            %strong
              unbounded differential graded
              = succeed "-ring" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{C}^\infty
            is a (homologically graded with arbitrary degrees) real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+differential+graded+algebra"} commutative differential graded algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with a structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-ring"} C^∞-ring
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                      %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0^c=\ker(A_0\to A_{-1})
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Every coconnective differential graded C^∞-ring in the sense defined above is also an unbounded differential graded C^∞-ring concentrated in nonpositive homological degrees, since the kernel of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-derivation"} C^∞-derivation
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-ring"} C^∞-ring
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-ring"} C^∞-ring
              structure on its 0-cocycles only, which is not enough to reconstruct a C^∞-structure on the whole degree 0 part or ensure that the degree 0 differential is a C^∞-derivation. The stronger condition is essential for some theorems about coconnective differential graded
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-rings"} C^∞-rings
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+differential+forms+form+the+free+C%5E%E2%88%9E-DGA+on+smooth+functions"} smooth differential forms form the free C^∞-DGA on smooth functions
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              In fact, having a C^∞-structure in degree 0 together an ordinary algebra structure in higher degrees is sufficient to define a C^∞-structure for nonhomogeneous elements of mixed degrees: given a smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x_1, \ldots, x_n)
              and some nonhomogeneous even elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_1, \ldots, b_n
              (i.e., formal sums of homogeneous elements of even degrees), set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_i = c_i + d_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_i
              has degree 0 and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_i
              is a sum of homogeneous elements of positive cohomological degree. Now set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi m
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi k
                    %msubsup
                      %mi d
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi m
                          %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mi k
                    %mo !
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(b_1, \ldots, b_n) = \sum_m (\partial_m f)(c_1, \ldots, c_n) \prod_k d_k^{m_k} / m_k!,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              is a multi-index and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi m
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_m f
              is the partial derivative of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1, \ldots, c_n
              using the C^∞-structure. This formula yields a (possibly infinite) formal sum of homogeneous elements. Nonhomogeneous elements of odd degrees can be incorporated in the picture by taking
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi R
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi R
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in \mathrm{C}^\infty(\mathbf{R}^m)\otimes\Lambda(\mathbf{R}^n)
              (i.e.,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              has
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              even and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              odd variables) and modifying the formula accordingly. This yields an equivalent notion of differential graded C^∞-rings. The price to pay is having to manipulate infinite formal sums of homogeneous elements, so we choose the equivalent definition given above, even though it may seem slightly ad hoc on the first sight.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Differential graded C^∞-rings can be equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure"} model structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transferred+model+structure"} transferred
            from the projective model structure on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            via the forgetful functor.
          %p
            Restricting to
            %strong connective
            differential graded C^∞-rings, the resulting model structure is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            of simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-rings"} C^∞-rings
            equipped with the model structure transferred along the forgetful functor to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            equipped with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan%E2%80%93Quillen+model+structure"} Kan–Quillen model structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normalized+chains"} normalized chains
            functor, which sends a simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-rings"} C^∞-rings
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normalized+chains"} normalized chains
            equipped with the induced structure of a differential graded C^∞-ring. This is analogous to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Dold%E2%80%93Kan+correspondence"} monoidal Dold–Kan correspondence
          %p
            In this form, the statement was first proved in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Taroyan"} Taroyan 2023
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Nuiten"} Nuiten 2018, Remark 2.2.11
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-rings"} C^∞-rings
            and does not explicitly identify the adjoint functors.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The essential ingredients (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+C%5E%E2%88%9E-differentials"} Kähler C^∞-differentials
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-derivations"} C^∞-derivations
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E.+J.+Dubuc"}> E. J. Dubuc
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+Kock"} A. Kock
              = succeed "," do
                %em On 1-form classifiers
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/00927878408823064"} doi:10.1080/00927878408823064
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://tildeweb.au.dk/au76680/DK84.pdf"} author pdf
          %p
            The earliest known occurrence of differential graded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-rings"} C^∞-rings
            is in the paper
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"}> Domenico Fiorenza
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
              = succeed "," do
                %em Čech cocycles for differential characteristic classes: an ∞-Lie theoretic construction
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.4735"} arXiv
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4310/atmp.2012.v16.n1.a5"} doi
          %p where at the bottom of page 28 in arXiv version 1 one reads:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The underlying algebra in degree 0 can be generalized to an algebra over some Lawvere theory. In particular in a proper setup of higher differential geometry, we would demand
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})_0
              to be equipped with the structure of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-ring"} C^∞-ring
          %p Additional references:
          %p
            On unbounded differential graded rings for arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermat+theories"} Fermat theories
            (including
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%5E%E2%88%9E-rings"} C^∞-rings
          %ul
            %li#CR
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Carchedi"}> David Carchedi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Roytenberg"} Dmitry Roytenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homological Algebra for Superalgebras of Differentiable Functions
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.3745"} arXiv
          %p
            On the equivalence of connective differential graded C^∞-rings and simplicial C^∞-rings via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normalized+chains"} normalized chains
            functor:
          %ul
            %li#Taroyan
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Taroyan"}> Gregory Taroyan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equivalent models of derived stacks
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.12699"} arXiv
          %p
            For a similar statement in which the category of
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            = succeed "-rings," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
          %ul
            %li#Nuiten
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joost+Nuiten"}> Joost Nuiten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lie algebroids in derived differential topology
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/1874/364151"} doi
